{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"01","chapter_title":"AI-Enhanced Presentation Development","title":"Presentation Purpose and Audience","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting presentation purpose and audience to presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development","cat100_supplement_presentation_purpose_and_audience","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_calibration"]},"content":"# Presentation Purpose and Audience\n\n![Presentation Purpose and Audience textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-01-presentation-purpose-and-audience.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost weak presentations fail before a single slide is designed, because the author never decided who the audience is or what that audience must be able to do afterward. A deck built for \"everyone\" persuades no one. The same data that should make a hiring panel say \"invite this candidate\" will bore a classmate and confuse a parent, because each of those audiences arrives with different questions, different prior knowledge, and a different decision to make.\n\nIn this section you learn to write a one-sentence purpose statement and a one-sentence audience profile before you open slide software, then to size and shape the deck to fit them. The artifact you produce, a presentation storyboard with an explicit audience constraint and slide-level evidence, is the place where those two sentences become visible and checkable by someone else.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTwo students submit decks on the same project. Maya writes at the top of her storyboard: \"Audience: the analyst-internship hiring panel (three working analysts, five minutes per candidate). Purpose: they should rank me in their top three to interview.\" Devon writes nothing; he opens PowerPoint and starts choosing a theme.\n\nMaya's first slide leads with \"Cleaned 4,000 rows and cut report time 40 percent,\" because that is what a hiring analyst weighs. Devon's first slide is a title slide with his name in a script font. Both decks are finished and both look fine. Only Maya can tell you what each slide is for, and only Maya's storyboard lets a reviewer check whether the slides actually serve the stated audience.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPurpose and audience are two separate decisions that people routinely collapse into one. **Purpose** is the action you want the audience to take: interview me, fund the referral feature, approve the budget, change the deadline. **Audience** is the specific reader and the constraints they bring: how many of them, how much time, how much background, and what would make them say no. A purpose without a named audience drifts into generic information dumping; an audience without a stated purpose drifts into \"here is everything I did.\"\n\nOnce both are fixed, they generate every later choice. Three properties of the audience set hard limits on the deck:\n\n| Audience property | What it constrains | Example consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Time available | Number of slides and depth | 3 minutes at roughly 1 slide/minute means about 3 slides, not 12 |\n| Prior knowledge | What you can assume vs. must explain | Working analysts need no \"what is a spreadsheet\" slide |\n| The decision they own | Which evidence belongs and which gets cut | A hiring panel weighs skill match, not your favorite class |\n\nThis is why \"audience first, evidence second, tool third\" is not a slogan. The audience determines which evidence is relevant, and only after the relevant evidence is chosen does the tool (theme, layout, AI assistant) have anything legitimate to do. The course anchor that does the heaviest work here is NACE career readiness, which asks whether the artifact would let a professional decision-maker act; the data-ink anchor matters later, once you know what the audience needs to see.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: build a 3-minute pitch deck for a campus hiring panel that should leave thinking \"invite this candidate.\"\n\n1. Name the audience and purpose in one line: *Audience = analyst-internship hiring panel; Purpose = earn an interview in 3 minutes.*\n2. Size the deck to the time. At roughly one minute per slide, that is 3 slides, not 12.\n3. Draft the storyboard so each slide answers a panel question:\n\n   | Slide | Panel question | Takeaway headline |\n   | --- | --- | --- |\n   | 1 | Who are you and why care? | \"I turned messy survey data into a decision the team used.\" |\n   | 2 | Can you do the work? | \"Cleaned 4,000 rows, cut report time 40%.\" |\n   | 3 | Do you fit this role? | \"These three skills match the internship posting.\" |\n\n4. Audit against the purpose line. A \"favorite class project\" slide does not help the panel decide to interview, so it is cut.\n\nPut the worked example in the right order. The whole point of \"audience first, evidence second, tool third\" is that each step depends on the one before it, so reorder these into the sequence Maya actually followed:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the audience and the action they should take in one line\", \"Size the deck to the audience time budget (about one slide per minute)\", \"Draft a per-slide question the audience needs answered\", \"Choose the evidence that answers each question\", \"Cut any slide that serves no audience question\", \"Apply theme, font, and layout last\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Devon opens PowerPoint and picks a theme before writing anything down. Which step of the correct sequence did he skip past?\" explanation=\"Theme selection is the last step. Devon jumped straight to the tool before naming the audience, the action, and the per-slide questions, so nothing constrains what goes on each slide.\" options='[{\"text\":\"He named the audience but skipped the purpose\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"He skipped naming the audience and purpose and jumped to the tool\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"He cut a slide too early\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"He sized the deck before choosing evidence\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe cut is the evidence of judgment. Anyone can add slides; the storyboard shows that you removed a slide because it did not serve the named audience. That is the difference between a deck that is finished and a deck that is aimed.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nSet the audience and purpose before any design choice, then justify every slide by the question it answers for that audience. A slide that does not move the audience toward the purpose is decoration, no matter how good it looks.\n\nBefore submitting the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, check whether the storyboard can answer three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** What single action should this audience take after the deck, and does the last slide ask for it?\n2. **Evidence check:** For each slide, which panel or audience question does it answer, and what number or example backs the headline?\n3. **Revision check:** Which slide did you cut or rewrite because it did not serve this audience, and why?\n\nIf you cannot name the audience question each slide answers, the deck is built for no one in particular.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating \"audience\" as a demographic label (\"college students\") rather than as a decision-maker with limited time and a specific call to make. A label tells you nothing about what to put on a slide; a decision and a time budget tell you almost everything.\n\nA second misreading is believing a longer, more detailed deck is safer because it \"covers more.\" Coverage is the opposite of purpose. Every extra slide spends the audience's limited attention and dilutes the one action you want.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply this to a real reassignment. Suppose your hiring-panel deck must now be sent as a self-running file to a busy recruiter who will skim it on a phone with no narration. The purpose can stay the same (earn an interview), but two audience properties just changed: there is no live time budget you control, and there is no voice to carry meaning. Your storyboard has to absorb that.\n\nYou would move the spoken context into the slide itself, so the headline \"Cut report time 40 percent\" now carries its own one-line proof beneath it, because no narrator will say it. You might add a single closing slide with explicit contact and availability, since the recruiter cannot ask you live. You are not redesigning for taste; you are re-deriving the deck from a changed audience constraint, which is exactly the skill the storyboard is meant to demonstrate.\n\nWalk the reassignment yourself. Your live three-slide panel deck just became a self-running file a busy recruiter skims on a phone. Decide what to re-derive first:\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The deck got reassigned to a phone-skimming recruiter\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The audience changed: no live time budget you control, no voice to carry meaning. What do you re-derive first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pick a flashier theme so it grabs attention on a small screen\",\"next\":\"theme\",\"feedback\":\"Theme is the last layer. You changed an audience constraint, not a taste preference, so the tool comes after the message is re-derived.\"},{\"label\":\"Re-check which audience properties changed (time control and narration), then fix the slides they break\",\"next\":\"rederive\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Two audience properties changed, so you re-derive the deck from them rather than redesigning for taste.\"},{\"label\":\"Add more slides since there is no live time limit\",\"next\":\"morelides\",\"feedback\":\"A phone-skimming recruiter has even less patience than a panel. More slides spends attention you no longer control.\"}]},\"rederive\":{\"prompt\":\"Good. The narration channel is gone. What concrete move follows from that?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Move the spoken proof into the slide itself, under the headline\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. No narrator will say the proof, so the headline must carry its own one-line evidence.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep the slides bare and assume the recruiter infers the proof\",\"next\":\"bare\",\"feedback\":\"Without a voice, an inferred proof is a missing proof. The slide must now state it.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"The headline carries its own proof and a closing slide adds contact and availability. The deck was re-derived from the changed constraint.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the target: re-derive from the audience, not redesign for taste.\",\"choices\":[]},\"theme\":{\"prompt\":\"Restart and re-derive from the audience instead.\",\"feedback\":\"Theme never fixes a constraint change.\",\"choices\":[]},\"morelides\":{\"prompt\":\"Restart and re-derive from the audience instead.\",\"feedback\":\"Coverage is the opposite of purpose.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bare\":{\"prompt\":\"Restart: with no voice, the proof must move onto the slide.\",\"feedback\":\"A missing narrator means the slide carries the proof.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, the audience question; on the right, the slide and evidence that answer it. If a slide has no audience question, flag it as a candidate to cut. By the end of 05.01, your note should let a reviewer reconstruct why each surviving slide exists.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Who is the specific decision-maker, and how much time do they have?\n2. What single action defines success?\n3. Which evidence does that audience actually weigh, and which did you cut?\n4. How would the deck change if the audience's time, knowledge, or decision changed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe storyboard is strong when a stranger can read your one-line audience and purpose statements, then verify that every slide earns its place against them. Treat theme and font as the last layer. The earlier, load-bearing layers are the audience definition, the per-slide question, and the cut you made.\n\nA useful note for the artifact would sound like this: \"I cut the 'favorite project' slide because the hiring panel weighs skill match, not enthusiasm, and at one minute per slide it would have crowded out the 40 percent result. I kept the AI's suggestion to add a metric to slide 2, but rejected its suggestion to add a fourth slide, because three minutes leaves no room for it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same content to a stricter setting: a 60-second elevator version for a career fair, spoken, with no slides at all. The purpose holds (earn a follow-up), but the constraint collapses from three slides to roughly 150 spoken words. You must now choose the single strongest piece of evidence and drop the rest. If you can explain which slide survives as your one sentence and why, you understand purpose and audience as a relationship you can re-derive, not a template you copied.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the ideas this section asks you to connect. Trace how naming the audience drives evidence-based revision of the storyboard:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_presentation_purpose_and_audience\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-01-presentation-purpose-and-audience-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to NACE career readiness and what a hiring decision-maker actually weighs.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a deck written for \"everyone\" with one written for a named three-person panel on a time budget.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same purpose-and-audience logic to a different decision-maker (recruiter, instructor, client).\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where naming the audience changed a specific slide.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence for CAT 100 05.01. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_presentation_purpose_and_audience\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the storyboard states the audience in one line, states the action you want in one line, gives every slide an audience question to answer, and records at least one slide you cut because it did not serve that audience.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_presentation_purpose_and_audience","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_presentation_purpose_and_audience","cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"],"stem":"Your slide deck must persuade a campus hiring panel to invite you to interview in a 3-minute slot. Which storyboard line shows the deck is built for that audience and purpose?","options":["Slide 1: 'About Me' with a full-screen photo and a decorative title font.","Slide 1 takeaway: 'Invite this candidate.' Slide 2: one quantified project result. Slide 3: skill-to-role match. Total 3 slides for 3 minutes.","Twelve slides covering every course project in chronological order.","A single slide of bullet points copied from the resume."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The audience (hiring panel) and purpose (earn an interview) drive a tight 3-slide scope sized to the 3-minute limit.","difficulty":"easy","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_one_size_deck"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You ask BigAL for help on this deck. Which note names the audience-purpose decision well enough for targeted feedback?","options":["This deck looks good to me.","My audience is a hiring panel and my purpose is an interview invite, but slide 4 explains a class assignment they will not care about. Should I cut it?","I am stuck.","Make it more professional."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong note names audience, purpose, and the specific slide that may not serve them, so feedback can be exact.","difficulty":"medium","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_audience_mismatch"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"What is a defensible way to use AI while setting purpose and audience for this deck?","options":["Ask AI to generate the whole deck and present it unchanged.","Ask AI to list questions a hiring panel might have, then you decide which one each slide answers.","Let AI invent an audience for you so you do not have to.","Use AI only to pick a slide theme color."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI surfaces candidate audience questions; you keep authorship of which question each slide actually serves.","difficulty":"hard","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_polish_over_fit"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_1_presentation_purpose_and_audience_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A reviewer says your purpose statement is vague. Which revision makes the purpose inspectable?","options":["'My purpose is to talk about my work.'","'After 3 minutes the hiring panel should be able to say why my data project qualifies me for the analyst internship.'","'My purpose is to use nice slides.'","'My purpose is to finish the assignment.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A testable purpose names the audience, the time, and the exact judgment they should be able to make.","difficulty":"medium"}]}}